import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Retro — the joint board: forward + backward on one e-graph","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"Retro — the joint board: forward + backward on one e-graph","type":"retro","date":"2026-10-14T00:00:00.000Z"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"graphopt/project/retros/joint-training-graph.md","filePath":"vendored/graphopt/project/retros/joint-training-graph.md"}`),a={name:`graphopt/project/retros/joint-training-graph.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="retro-—-the-joint-board-forward-backward-on-one-e-graph" tabindex="-1">Retro — the joint board: forward + backward on one e-graph <a class="header-anchor" href="#retro-—-the-joint-board-forward-backward-on-one-e-graph" aria-label="Permalink to “Retro — the joint board: forward + backward on one e-graph”">​</a></h1><p>Plan 0021&#39;s meta-arena gave the board a <code>declare</code>/<code>handle</code> action space; <code>training.py</code>&#39;s reverse handler made gradient programs ordinary terms. <code>joint_probe</code> asks the natural follow-up: the autograd pipeline is <em>two</em> programs (forward graph, backward graph) that can never share work across the boundary — does one e-graph over forward + every gradient root do better?</p><p>Reproduce:</p><pre><code>.venv/bin/python -m catopt_discovery.training --joint
</code></pre><h2 id="the-measurement" tabindex="-1">The measurement <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-measurement" aria-label="Permalink to “The measurement”">​</a></h2><p>Per <code>_demo_forwards</code> case: <code>backward</code> derives every leaf&#39;s gradient term under a shaped all-ones cotangent. The <em>separate</em> arm saturates the forward in one <code>EGraph</code> and the whole gradient program in a second, billing the two extracted programs independently — the autograd comparison, where cross-program sharing is impossible. The <em>joint</em> arm adds every root to one e-graph and saturates once; <code>dag_cost</code> dedups shared subtrees, so <code>joint &lt; separate</code> is measured cross-boundary sharing.</p><p><code>count_cost</code>, <code>DEFAULT</code> rules, max_nodes=20k:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>forward</th><th style="text-align:right;">separate</th><th style="text-align:right;">joint</th><th style="text-align:right;">shared e-classes</th><th>enodes sep/joint</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>silu_fwd</td><td style="text-align:right;">10.0</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>10.0</strong></td><td style="text-align:right;">2</td><td>17/15</td></tr><tr><td>softmax_fwd</td><td style="text-align:right;">13.0</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>13.0</strong></td><td style="text-align:right;">3</td><td>21/18</td></tr><tr><td>linear_fwd</td><td style="text-align:right;">13.0</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>10.0</strong></td><td style="text-align:right;">6</td><td>27/21</td></tr></tbody></table><p><code>linear_fwd</code> wins 23%: tanh&#39;s VJP spec spells <code>1 - tanh(a0)^2</code>, so the <em>entire forward term</em> is a subterm of all three gradient roots — in one e-graph it is one e-class, extracted once, billed once. In two pipelines it is paid twice (once as the forward, once inside each gradient program). Same for <code>matmul(x,w)</code> shared between <code>dx = g·W^T</code>&#39;s siblings.</p><p>The enodes column is the second win, quieter but unconditional: one saturation over the union costs fewer enodes than two saturations (17→15, 21→18, 27→21) because shared subterms have a shared rewrite frontier.</p><h2 id="the-honest-wrinkle-—-fusion-hides-sharing" tabindex="-1">The honest wrinkle — fusion hides sharing <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-honest-wrinkle-—-fusion-hides-sharing" aria-label="Permalink to “The honest wrinkle — fusion hides sharing”">​</a></h2><p>silu/softmax are <em>flat</em> despite 2–3 shared e-classes. Cause: <code>silu_fold</code>/<code>softmax_fold</code> fuse the forward into <code>silu(x)</code> / <code>softmax(x)</code> — locally optimal (−1 op) — but the fused member no longer <em>contains</em> <code>sigmoid(x)</code>/<code>exp(x)</code> as a visible subterm, so the backward&#39;s spelled reuse can&#39;t dedup against it. Greedy per-class extraction picks the fused member because it prices each class in isolation.</p><p>Without the folds (<code>rules=DEFAULT - {silu_fold, softmax_fold}</code>) the joint arm wins both: silu 11→10, softmax 15→13. So the sharing is real — but see below: forcing the spelled member back only ties, so the fold is a <em>wash</em>, not a failure.</p><h2 id="the-named-lever-—-measured-a-wash" tabindex="-1">The named lever — measured: a wash <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-named-lever-—-measured-a-wash" aria-label="Permalink to “The named lever — measured: a wash”">​</a></h2><p>The codebase already has the mechanism: <code>extract_best</code>&#39;s <code>overrides</code> / <code>extract_paired</code> — built for the fused-GEMM-sharing case where k members each selecting <code>split_i(fused)</code> must coordinate to bill <code>fused</code> once. The joint board needs the dual: <em>unfold</em> the forward&#39;s fused member to expose a subterm the backward pays for anyway.</p><p><strong>Measured, and it ties.</strong> Forcing the spelled <code>mul</code>/<code>div</code> member of the forward root (<code>overrides={fwd_class: spelled_enode}</code>) leaves silu at 10 and softmax at 13 — identical to greedy. The math is exact under <code>count_cost</code>: fused <code>silu(x)</code> saves precisely the one op that sharing <code>sigmoid(x)</code> would have saved. For <em>one-use</em> sharing, fusion ≡ sharing and coordination buys nothing.</p><p>The structural win is <em>multi-use</em> sharing: <code>linear_fwd</code>&#39;s <code>tanh</code> term is consumed by the forward plus all three gradients (4 consumers), so billing it once saves 3 copies — and greedy extraction already captures it because the shared form is the class optimum. The general rule the probe supports: <strong>joint saturations pay when a subterm has ≥3 consumers across the boundary; folds and one-use shares are a wash.</strong> A cost model that prices launches/memory rather than op count could break the tie differently — <code>flops_cost</code> is the obvious next column.</p><h2 id="caveats" tabindex="-1">Caveats <a class="header-anchor" href="#caveats" aria-label="Permalink to “Caveats”">​</a></h2><ul><li>Extraction correctness is pinned, not assumed: joint-extracted gradient terms evaluate numerically equal to <code>torch.autograd</code> (<code>test_joint_extraction_stays_correct</code>, the linear case — the one that shares hardest).</li><li>The separate arm is the <em>strong</em> baseline: it bills the gradient program as one DAG (all gradient roots dedup among themselves), which is more than torch actually does at the FX level. Joint still wins or ties everywhere.</li><li><code>shared</code> counts e-classes reachable from both the forward and gradient roots — an upper bound on exploitable sharing, not all of it billable under greedy extraction (see the wrinkle).</li><li>Demo corpus is three hand-shaped cases (silu, softmax spine, linear+tanh). The claim needs a real module sweep next — <code>TorchSource</code> already produces such terms (the autograd end-to-end test does this for <code>backward</code> alone).</li></ul>`,19)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};